# Cline Rules

## Core
- Minimize tokens, tool calls, and context usage.
- Gather only the information required to complete the task.
- Prefer modifying existing code over introducing new abstractions.
- Follow existing project patterns and conventions.

## Discovery
- Prefer targeted search before reading files; read surrounding context (±20 lines) before patching to avoid breaking adjacent logic.
- Read only the necessary parts of files.
- Avoid generated, build, vendor, and unrelated files.

## Changes
- Make the smallest change that solves the problem.
- Verify functions, APIs, and assumptions before using them.
- Avoid unrelated refactors, formatting changes, file moves, or new dependencies unless required.
- Never modify .env, secrets, or config files without presenting a diff first.
- For irreversible changes (DB migrations, schema changes, proto updates): present the full plan and wait for explicit confirmation before executing.
- Treat contract files (CLAUDE.md, env interface specs, shared API definitions) as read-only unless explicitly instructed; present a diff and wait for confirmation.

## Commands
- Avoid large outputs; truncate or summarize when output exceeds ~50 lines.
- Use RTK for commands likely to produce substantial output (build logs, test suites, installs).
- Use timeouts for potentially blocking operations.
- Never run test or build commands without output control (e.g., redirect to file or capped output), to prevent blocking or hanging sessions.
- Prefer one stream at a time for debugging runs.
- Prefer decomposed execution steps for debugging when a command includes initialization, external libraries, or runtime systems (e.g. simulators, ML frameworks, rendering engines).
- Avoid chaining multiple initialization-sensitive operations into a single one-liner when diagnosing issues; isolate stages to identify blocking points.
- When using Docker (docker run, docker compose run), prefer non-interactive execution (-T) unless interaction is explicitly required.
- Assume that environment initialization (GUI, physics engines, GPU contexts, rendering backends) may block silently; validate initialization separately from runtime logic.

## Validation
- Run the smallest validation that provides confidence.
- Avoid full-project test runs unless explicitly requested.
- If modules are known to have coupled tests or side effects, escalate validation scope and flag it.

## Dependencies
- Never add, upgrade, or remove dependencies without listing the change and its rationale first.
- Check for version conflicts before installing; flag silent incompatibilities (e.g. transitive deps).

## Git
- Review status and diff before major changes.
- Never overwrite or revert user changes without instruction.